{"name":"Sensual Peach","hex":"#ffd2b6","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sensual","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Inducing pleasurable or erotic sensations.","Of or pertaining to the physical senses; sensory."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sensual"},{"term":"peach","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any tree of species Prunus persica, native to China and now widely cultivated throughout temperate regions, having pink flowers and edible fruit.","Soft juicy stone fruit of the peach tree, having yellow flesh, downy, red-tinted yellow skin, and a deeply sculptured pit or stone containing a single seed."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the color peach.","Particularly pleasing or agreeable."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/peach"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sense","blocks":[{"text":"A sense is a biological system used by an organism for sensation, the process of gathering information about the surroundings through the detection of stimuli. During sensation, sense organs collect various stimuli for transduction, meaning transformation into a form that can be understood by the brain. Sensation and perception are fundamental to nearly every aspect of an organism's cognition, behavior and thought."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_The_Five_Senses.png/330px-XBio_illustration_%E2%80%93_The_Five_Senses.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sense"},{"title":"Peach","blocks":[{"text":"The peach is a deciduous tree that bears edible juicy fruits with various characteristics. Most are simply called peaches, while the glossy-skinned, non-fuzzy varieties are called nectarines. Though from the same species, they are regarded commercially as different fruits."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg/330px-Illustration_Prunus_persica_clean_no_descr.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach"},{"title":"Peach (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Peach is a color that is named for the pale color of the interior flesh of the peach fruit. Like the color apricot, the color peach is paler than most actual peach fruits and seems to have been formulated primarily to create a pastel palette of colors for interior design."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peach_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/SensoryProcessing.png/500px-SensoryProcessing.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/SensoryProcessing.png/500px-SensoryProcessing.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sensation consists of signal collection and transduction.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASensoryProcessing.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Philippe_Mercier_-_The_Sense_of_Taste_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Philippe_Mercier_-_The_Sense_of_Taste_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Philippe_Mercier_-_The_Sense_of_Taste_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-Philippe_Mercier_-_The_Sense_of_Taste_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Philippe_Mercier_-_The_Sense_of_Taste_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Philippe_Mercier_-_The_Sense_of_Taste_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Sense of Taste by Philippe Mercier c. 1744–1747","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fi